22:29:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a wonderfully intuitive question, and it’s exactly where the confusion usually takes root. It *feels* like if the far side never turns to face us, it must always be facing away from the light, too. But the key is this: **what direction the Moon faces relative to Earth is completely separate from what direction it faces relative to the Sun.** Think of it with a simple analogy: Imagine you and a friend are standing in a large, dark room with a single bright lamp in the middle. Now, hold hands and start walking in a circle around the lamp, but you always keep your face turned toward your friend—never looking away. Your back is to your friend all the time, just like the far side of the Moon is always turned away from Earth. But notice: as you both walk around the lamp, sometimes the lamp is behind your friend, shining directly onto your face. At that point, your friend (Earth) sees your face all lit up—that’s a “full moon” for them. At that same moment, your back (the far side) is in total darkness. Then, half an orbit later, you’re on the opposite side of the lamp. Now the lamp is *behind you*, shining directly onto your back. Your friend sees your face completely in shadow (“new moon”), but your back is in full daylight. In other words, simply because you always face your friend doesn’t mean you never turn your back to the lamp. You do—twice per orbit—because your path around the room naturally sweeps your back through the light. The Moon works exactly the same way. It’s tidally locked to Earth (always showing us the same face), but it orbits Earth, and the Earth–Moon system orbits the Sun. That orbital motion means the Sun appears to circle the Moon, bringing a two-week-long day and a two-week-long night to every side—including the far side. - At **new moon** (when the Moon is between Earth and Sun), the near side is dark and the far side is fully illuminated—it's "lunchtime" there. - At **full moon**, the near side is fully lit and